{"name":"White Lie","hex":"#dededc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white lie","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deliberately untrue statement which is intended to produce a favorable result; especially, one which is judged to be better than an overhonest alternative, as for example to remain polite in a superficial social exchange, or to pick one's battles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_lie"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Lie (film)","blocks":[{"text":"White Lie is a 2019 Canadian drama film written and directed by Yonah Lewis and Calvin Thomas. The film stars Kacey Rohl as Katie Arneson, a university student who fakes a cancer diagnosis for the attention and financial gain, but gets caught up in having to maintain her lie."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/White_Lie_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Lie_(film)"},{"title":"Lie","blocks":[{"text":"A lie is an assertion that is believed to be false, typically used with the intention of deceiving or misleading someone. The practice of communicating lies is called lying. A person who communicates a lie may be termed a liar. Lies can be interpreted as deliberately false statements or misleading statements, though not all statements that are literally false are considered lies – metaphors, hyperboles, and other figurative rhetoric are not intended to mislead, while lies are explicitly meant for literal interpretation by their audience. Lies may also serve a variety of instrumental, interpersonal, or psychological functions for the individuals who use them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Pinocchio.jpg/330px-Pinocchio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lie"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg/500px-%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg/1280px-%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg/500px-%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A motivational poster about lying declares \"An ostrich only thinks he 'covers up'\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf/page1-500px-How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf/page1-1280px-How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf/page1-500px-How_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Infographic How to spot fake news published by the International Federation of Library Associations and Institutions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHow_to_Spot_Fake_News.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/CDCA_auto_repair_notice.jpg/500px-CDCA_auto_repair_notice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/CDCA_auto_repair_notice.jpg/500px-CDCA_auto_repair_notice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Consumer protection laws often mandate the posting of notices, such as this one which appears in all automotive repair shops in California.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACDCA_auto_repair_notice.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg/500px-Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg/1280px-Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg/500px-Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plato presented arguments to justify the use of noble lies in his Republic.[26]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/